## 1. Project Identity & Strategic Context

NoteDock is an MIT-licensed local browser experiment for freelance video editors and small
post-production teams. Its measurable success criterion is turning scattered pasted notes into one
reviewable and exportable checklist while preserving source, reviewer, timecode, ambiguity, and
untimed input. The design favors deterministic behavior, local data handling, minimal dependency
surface, responsive accessibility, and honest proof boundaries.

## 2. Current System State & Architecture Map

Fully implemented:

- Plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript UI at `index.html`, `styles.css`, and `src/app.js`.
- Deterministic domain logic at `src/parser.js`.
- Local development server at `scripts/serve.mjs`.
- Audit tooling at `scripts/audit.mjs`.
- Node unit tests at `tests/parser.test.js`.
- Playwright workflow and proof suites under `playwright/`.
- Six durable screenshots under `proof/screenshots/`.
- README, security policy, contributing guide, changelog, license, product docs, and completion doc.

Partially built or broken: none known. GitHub publication is the only pending release step.

Deliberately deferred: accounts, upload, media hosting, transcription, AI inference, NLE sync,
client approvals, collaboration, analytics, payments, provider integrations, and hosted
deployment.

Flow: pasted text → source-header/timecode parser → stable chronological grouping → source-preserved
checklist → local Markdown clipboard or CSV download.

## 3. Critical Decisions & Reasoning History

- Chose scattered revision-note normalization after screening ten candidates and qualifying the
  strongest against eight public sources.
- Chose a static app because the outcome does not justify React runtime complexity.
- Kept parsing deterministic and visible; ambiguous notes remain visible rather than guessed.
- Positioned the app as a rescue bridge for notes outside Frame.io, Vimeo Review, or Filestage, not
  as another media-review portal.
- Removed a non-permissive testing dependency and used Playwright-native accessibility assertions.
- Limited monetization to a documented hosted-team hypothesis with no demand claim.

